1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Their customized ships cut through the oceans like sharks.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:08,000
From their use of big guns, to hand grenades, to navigation,

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
the technology they developed to practice their murderous trade

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,000
helped revolutionize naval warfare.

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Now, learn the three R's of pirate tech on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:53,843 --> 00:00:57,160
Resting 20 feet beneath the surface of North Carolina's

7
00:00:57,161 --> 00:01:00,000
Beaufort Inlet, researchers believe they found

8
00:01:00,001 --> 00:01:02,200
remains of the Queen Anne's Revenge.

9
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:16,180
This legendary vessel was once the flagship of the notorious

10
00:01:16,181 --> 00:01:18,760
pirate Blackbeard.

11
00:01:18,761 --> 00:01:23,460
Blackbeard was probably one of the foremost pirates of his

12
00:01:23,461 --> 00:01:28,320
era and certainly today is an icon of piracy.

13
00:01:28,321 --> 00:01:29,740
I think it was a little of everything

14
00:01:29,741 --> 00:01:33,200
that made Blackbeard the pirate that he was,

15
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:37,300
operating essentially a small flotilla of ships,

16
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:41,120
anywhere from 300 to 400 pirates under his command

17
00:01:41,121 --> 00:01:43,080
at any one particular time.

18
00:01:43,660 --> 00:01:46,120
No one knows for sure how the ship sank.

19
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:50,160
One theory speculates it was not during battle,

20
00:01:50,161 --> 00:01:52,500
but on a routine maintenance voyage.

21
00:01:52,501 --> 00:01:59,600
he brought his flotilla to Beaufort Inlet

22
00:01:59,601 --> 00:02:01,540
he tried to get the Queen of his Revenge

23
00:02:01,541 --> 00:02:03,340
into the inlet so he could clean her bottom

24
00:02:03,341 --> 00:02:05,600
but in the process of trying to get her in

25
00:02:05,601 --> 00:02:07,400
she got stuck on a sandbar

26
00:02:07,401 --> 00:02:10,080
and he had no choice after trying to get her off

27
00:02:10,081 --> 00:02:11,620
he had no choice but to abandon her

28
00:02:11,621 --> 00:02:13,120
right where she had gotten stuck

29
00:02:13,121 --> 00:02:15,680
dead men tell no tales

30
00:02:15,681 --> 00:02:18,720
and Blackbeard's been dead for nearly 300 years

31
00:02:18,721 --> 00:02:22,460
but knowledge gained from a shipwreck like this

32
00:02:22,461 --> 00:02:25,320
can speak volumes about the technology of pirates

33
00:02:25,321 --> 00:02:28,100
and how they went about their buccaneering business.

34
00:02:29,660 --> 00:02:33,240
Each artifact from the wreck is carefully analyzed and cleaned

35
00:02:33,241 --> 00:02:35,220
after being brought to the surface.

36
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:38,480
And each has a story to tell.

37
00:02:41,540 --> 00:02:44,200
We have discovered 24 cannon on site.

38
00:02:44,420 --> 00:02:45,640
We have recovered eight.

39
00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:47,880
And we do think we have a few more areas

40
00:02:47,881 --> 00:02:49,420
that need to be investigated on the site

41
00:02:49,421 --> 00:02:50,920
that could possibly have a cannon.

42
00:02:50,921 --> 00:02:54,580
This cannon is possibly loaded. That is a good possibility.

43
00:02:54,581 --> 00:02:57,780
The four out of five that we have inspected have been loaded.

44
00:02:58,660 --> 00:03:02,220
Another aspect of these cannons that come off these shipwrecks

45
00:03:02,221 --> 00:03:05,820
that give us a clue that they might be pirates is that they're preloaded.

46
00:03:06,280 --> 00:03:10,680
These vessels sank with their charges still in the guns,

47
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:18,040
and most of the time regular vessels would not sail around with the cannons already loaded.

48
00:03:18,041 --> 00:03:22,980
In their day, pirate ships were the most feared vessels at sea.

49
00:03:26,280 --> 00:03:31,080
Elusive and fast, they were the souped-up, customized hot rods of the world's oceans.

50
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:37,740
And like many hot rods, they were hot in more ways than one, for most of them were stolen.

51
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:43,260
When a pirate captured a ship, and that's how virtually all of them acquired their ships,

52
00:03:43,261 --> 00:03:44,480
was by capturing.

53
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:48,260
They would strip off anything that didn't suit their purposes.

54
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:51,260
If there were extra compartments or extra cabins

55
00:03:51,820 --> 00:03:54,280
or extra bulkheads that were dividing up the hold,

56
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:55,780
they stripped all those out.

57
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:57,360
They just got in the way,

58
00:03:57,660 --> 00:04:01,400
and they didn't allow them to hold as much cargo as they wanted.

59
00:04:02,060 --> 00:04:05,300
This stripping also served to make the vessel lean and mean.

60
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:07,440
Every lost pound counted,

61
00:04:08,020 --> 00:04:11,020
because the lighter the ship was, the faster it would go.

62
00:04:11,021 --> 00:04:15,020
When possible, pirates added extra sails.

63
00:04:15,120 --> 00:04:18,020
If it was a one master or a two master,

64
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:21,960
they would then go and add additional yard and sail,

65
00:04:22,060 --> 00:04:25,760
so that they would have as much canvas as they could,

66
00:04:25,860 --> 00:04:27,600
so when they would catch the wind,

67
00:04:27,700 --> 00:04:31,280
that they could actually sail faster.

68
00:04:31,380 --> 00:04:34,640
The most prized ships were those with shallow drafts,

69
00:04:34,740 --> 00:04:37,340
such as sloops and schooners.

70
00:04:37,341 --> 00:04:38,700
schooners.

71
00:04:38,701 --> 00:04:41,080
This allowed them to go into shallow water,

72
00:04:41,081 --> 00:04:44,120
where ships with deep draft hulls couldn't pursue them.

73
00:04:44,121 --> 00:04:47,840
Pirates operated best with smaller vessels.

74
00:04:47,841 --> 00:04:48,960
It was a common sense thing.

75
00:04:48,961 --> 00:04:52,540
Larger, more powerful, heavily armed naval vessels

76
00:04:52,541 --> 00:04:55,020
could not follow them into those areas.

77
00:04:55,021 --> 00:04:59,700
And so it was a protection factor involved with that.

78
00:04:59,701 --> 00:05:03,860
Next, the pirates maximized the vessel's firepower.

79
00:05:03,861 --> 00:05:06,160
Typically, when they took a ship,

80
00:05:06,161 --> 00:05:07,320
They would add more cannons.

81
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:10,100
Many ships may have only had 10 to 12 cannons.

82
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:11,980
The pirates would tend to load them up.

83
00:05:12,740 --> 00:05:14,880
There are some who say that Queen Anne's Revenge

84
00:05:14,881 --> 00:05:16,940
had as many as 40 cannon on board,

85
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:18,860
so firepower was very important.

86
00:05:21,920 --> 00:05:24,820
Pirates had been honing their nefarious craft for centuries.

87
00:05:25,600 --> 00:05:28,420
The crime of piracy goes back before recorded history.

88
00:05:29,880 --> 00:05:34,040
You've had pirates as long as you've had people going to sea.

89
00:05:34,041 --> 00:05:39,040
Piracy is by definition just thievery at sea.

90
00:05:39,140 --> 00:05:42,840
The Minoans and the Phoenicians, the Egyptians had problems.

91
00:05:42,940 --> 00:05:46,780
The Greeks had such a problem with pirates that with the city

92
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:51,400
states actually started hiring pirates to collect taxes

93
00:05:51,500 --> 00:05:54,000
because they couldn't control them and felt they could

94
00:05:54,100 --> 00:05:55,700
control them that way.

95
00:05:55,800 --> 00:06:01,400
The Romans used the threat of piracy to actually even help

96
00:06:01,401 --> 00:06:04,700
build their empire because they would go to cities, states,

97
00:06:04,701 --> 00:06:08,640
and towns and say, we will help you rid yourself of pirates if

98
00:06:08,641 --> 00:06:12,640
you went into the Roman Empire.

99
00:06:12,641 --> 00:06:15,740
During the 8th century, the Vikings of Scandinavia

100
00:06:15,741 --> 00:06:19,060
became the master pirates of the seas.

101
00:06:19,061 --> 00:06:21,860
Their main innovation was the longship.

102
00:06:21,861 --> 00:06:25,760
With its shallow draft, it was the perfect hit-and-run vessel,

103
00:06:25,761 --> 00:06:30,200
which is the hallmark of any good pirate ship.

104
00:06:30,201 --> 00:06:34,200
Columbus's journey to the New World in 1492

105
00:06:34,300 --> 00:06:38,140
cleared the way for a golden age of piracy.

106
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:40,540
Pirates were the ultimate opportunists,

107
00:06:40,640 --> 00:06:43,180
and from the 16th century through the 18th,

108
00:06:43,280 --> 00:06:45,320
no waters offered more opportunity

109
00:06:45,420 --> 00:06:47,720
than those of the Americas.

110
00:06:47,820 --> 00:06:52,720
The Caribbean was the prime hotbed of pirate activity

111
00:06:52,820 --> 00:06:57,300
for the simple reason that's where the Spanish found all

112
00:06:57,301 --> 00:07:00,300
all the gold from the natives.

113
00:07:00,301 --> 00:07:04,140
And they went to South America and the Caribbean

114
00:07:04,141 --> 00:07:10,140
and found gold and silver and emeralds and pearls,

115
00:07:10,141 --> 00:07:13,540
and they started shipping a fleet of their galleons

116
00:07:13,541 --> 00:07:16,360
from South America back to Spain.

117
00:07:16,361 --> 00:07:21,460
So the ships were sitting ducks, loaded with all this treasure.

118
00:07:21,461 --> 00:07:24,360
The wealth carried by even one Spanish treasure galleon

119
00:07:24,361 --> 00:07:27,300
was staggering.

120
00:07:27,301 --> 00:07:32,300
In 1969, off the Florida coast, undersea explorer Mel Fisher

121
00:07:32,301 --> 00:07:37,980
discovered the wrecks of two Spanish galleons that sank during a hurricane in September 1622.

122
00:07:39,300 --> 00:07:44,960
The treasure they contained has an estimated value of $200 to $400 million.

123
00:07:46,900 --> 00:07:51,700
A portion of it is housed in Fisher's Museum, located in Key West, Florida.

124
00:07:51,701 --> 00:07:57,400
The ultimate goal of any pirate was to capture a Spanish treasure galleon.

125
00:07:57,401 --> 00:07:59,580
If they did that, they'd be set for life.

126
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:06,000
They would, on one of those ships, find all sorts of wonderful things like you see here.

127
00:08:06,460 --> 00:08:12,660
These are objects recovered from the Spanish galleon Nuestra Señora de Atocha, wrecked in 1622.

128
00:08:13,220 --> 00:08:17,280
You have silver ingots. These came from the mountains of the Andes.

129
00:08:17,281 --> 00:08:19,440
gold from South America,

130
00:08:19,660 --> 00:08:23,060
elephants' tusks acquired in the African slave trade.

131
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:26,380
Also included were the legendary pieces of eight,

132
00:08:26,880 --> 00:08:28,620
which are Spanish silver pesos.

133
00:08:30,620 --> 00:08:32,860
Sometimes the treasure was so overwhelming,

134
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:36,300
pirates had to re-ballast their ships in order to carry it.

135
00:08:37,160 --> 00:08:38,540
This stuff is heavy.

136
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:42,980
One weighs probably 80 pounds alone.

137
00:08:42,981 --> 00:08:46,680
Multiply that by 1,000 times on a galleon,

138
00:08:46,681 --> 00:08:49,520
and you can see how it was a full load.

139
00:08:49,820 --> 00:08:52,500
If you were to steal this and take it on board your vessel,

140
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:54,900
you'd have to take into account that weight.

141
00:08:54,901 --> 00:08:57,500
And so pirates, if they were lucky enough

142
00:08:57,501 --> 00:09:00,200
to find a cargo of silver ingots or coins,

143
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:03,280
they would actually have to get rid of some of their stone ballast

144
00:09:03,281 --> 00:09:04,520
or even some of their cannons.

145
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:07,580
Then they could replace that weight with heavy silver.

146
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:10,300
As thick as thieves,

147
00:09:10,820 --> 00:09:14,000
the bonds formed between the men in the pirate ranks were so strong

148
00:09:14,001 --> 00:09:16,280
that they became a separate society.

149
00:09:16,680 --> 00:09:21,680
often referring to themselves as the Brethren of the Coast.

150
00:09:21,780 --> 00:09:25,680
The Brethren of the Coast referred to sort of a grouping

151
00:09:25,780 --> 00:09:28,420
of pirates that found that through mutual cooperation,

152
00:09:28,520 --> 00:09:30,120
they could achieve much more.

153
00:09:30,220 --> 00:09:32,020
Pirates had a code of conduct.

154
00:09:32,120 --> 00:09:34,540
Each ship had a series of agreements

155
00:09:34,640 --> 00:09:37,240
that crew members had to sign on to.

156
00:09:37,340 --> 00:09:41,180
A lot of these were very basic, very common sense type rules,

157
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:45,040
no bringing women on board the ship,

158
00:09:45,041 --> 00:09:47,480
No arguing aboard ship.

159
00:09:48,720 --> 00:09:51,940
This sophistication extended to their form of government,

160
00:09:52,820 --> 00:09:53,520
democracy.

161
00:09:54,280 --> 00:09:57,260
We normally don't look at pirates in that light,

162
00:09:57,440 --> 00:10:01,800
but these guys would vote essentially on anything and everything

163
00:10:01,801 --> 00:10:03,280
that they were going to be doing.

164
00:10:04,060 --> 00:10:08,140
Ships they would attack, areas they would go into to operate.

165
00:10:08,920 --> 00:10:11,400
The captains of most ships were elected.

166
00:10:12,020 --> 00:10:14,620
The crew chose who their captain would be.

167
00:10:14,621 --> 00:10:17,220
Usually, when a ship first went out,

168
00:10:17,320 --> 00:10:21,800
the captain would be the person who would start the ship voyage.

169
00:10:21,900 --> 00:10:25,800
However, if the crew did not like the captain,

170
00:10:25,900 --> 00:10:31,040
if he was not getting them close to any treasure or foods,

171
00:10:31,140 --> 00:10:35,200
they were able to, by a majority vote, vote him out.

172
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:38,580
If the man was smart, he wouldn't object.

173
00:10:38,680 --> 00:10:41,580
Those who did paid.

174
00:10:41,680 --> 00:10:44,620
Along with their special code of conduct,

175
00:10:44,621 --> 00:10:48,440
Pirates also had a symbol, one that's still recognizable today,

176
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:50,440
the skull and crossbones.

177
00:10:51,440 --> 00:10:53,700
It was a potent psychological tool.

178
00:10:55,300 --> 00:11:00,020
Pirates began to integrate images that they found in graveyards,

179
00:11:00,220 --> 00:11:04,380
images that would strike fear into the hearts of the vessels they were attacking.

180
00:11:05,020 --> 00:11:09,200
You would see a skull and crossbones, which would symbolize death.

181
00:11:09,201 --> 00:11:14,180
You might see an hourglass, which symbolized you only have a short time

182
00:11:14,181 --> 00:11:15,220
before we attack.

183
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:18,240
And really the whole purpose of the pirate flag

184
00:11:18,241 --> 00:11:21,580
was to strike fear into the captain and the crew

185
00:11:21,581 --> 00:11:23,460
of the ship that they were going to attack.

186
00:11:24,440 --> 00:11:26,540
Flags on pirate ships also served

187
00:11:26,541 --> 00:11:28,240
as effective instruments of deception.

188
00:11:29,040 --> 00:11:31,580
One of the tactics that the pirates used

189
00:11:31,581 --> 00:11:33,000
to attack another vessel

190
00:11:33,001 --> 00:11:37,000
was they would have a variety of different national flags

191
00:11:37,001 --> 00:11:39,880
and depending what ship they were coming up against,

192
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:42,600
they would hoist up a flag of a friendly nation.

193
00:11:42,601 --> 00:11:43,980
When they got close enough,

194
00:11:43,981 --> 00:11:47,220
they would normally pull that down and put up a pirate flag,

195
00:11:47,600 --> 00:11:51,360
hoping that that would scare the ship and that they would not resist.

196
00:11:52,600 --> 00:11:57,760
During times of battle, the pirates would multipurpose their ship's parts and tools.

197
00:11:58,700 --> 00:12:02,300
There are many items used for work and everyday use on board the ship

198
00:12:02,301 --> 00:12:04,300
that can be turned into fairly lethal weapons.

199
00:12:05,020 --> 00:12:06,660
One of these is the belaying pin.

200
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:08,820
Now this thing is all over the boat.

201
00:12:09,120 --> 00:12:10,980
They're up and down the rails, around the mast.

202
00:12:10,981 --> 00:12:15,480
They're used for tying off lines, ropes, in the normal sailing of the boat.

203
00:12:15,481 --> 00:12:18,680
But once you undo those lines, pull them out of the hole,

204
00:12:18,681 --> 00:12:24,580
you've got a fairly vicious club or a pretty lethal projectile if you heave it fast enough.

205
00:12:24,581 --> 00:12:26,680
The cargo hook.

206
00:12:26,681 --> 00:12:31,680
Also found all over the boat for the men to work with hauling cargo in and out of the hole,

207
00:12:31,681 --> 00:12:34,280
clearing away wreckage or damaged rigging.

208
00:12:34,281 --> 00:12:38,480
Held in the proper position, this can be turned into a fairly lethal weapon.

209
00:12:39,440 --> 00:12:45,160
The boarding axe, used for cutting away damaged lines in a storm or some other natural occurrence.

210
00:12:45,161 --> 00:12:49,280
It has a long enough handle to be wielded very lethally.

211
00:12:49,640 --> 00:12:52,180
The pick-end, sharp axe-type end.

212
00:12:52,360 --> 00:12:53,580
It's a very nasty weapon.

213
00:12:54,440 --> 00:12:56,840
Each of these were very effective weapons.

214
00:12:57,300 --> 00:13:03,820
But on a pirate ship, the real intimidation factor was its firepower, especially the big guns.

215
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:13,180
A piece of eight was legal tender in the American colonies, and later the United States.

216
00:13:13,860 --> 00:13:17,580
Worth one dollar, it was demonetized in 1857.

217
00:13:18,720 --> 00:13:21,380
Pirate Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

218
00:13:25,120 --> 00:13:29,340
Today, the big guns brought up from the wreck of the ship believed to be Blackbeard's,

219
00:13:29,341 --> 00:13:32,980
the Queen Anne's revenge, are being meticulously restored.

220
00:13:38,480 --> 00:13:50,280
Approximately 10% of the ship's artifacts have been recovered since its discovery in 1996.

221
00:13:56,380 --> 00:14:00,480
No single item has been found to conclusively prove the ship's identity,

222
00:14:01,160 --> 00:14:06,460
but accumulated information suggests that the wreck is indeed that of Blackbeard's flagship.

223
00:14:08,480 --> 00:14:11,820
How could we know whether it was the Queen Anne's Revenge or not?

224
00:14:12,260 --> 00:14:16,620
Well, we have some descriptions of what the Queen Anne's Revenge was like.

225
00:14:16,960 --> 00:14:21,540
We know it sank in 1718, and we can look at this wreck.

226
00:14:21,880 --> 00:14:25,900
It's in the right place at the right time.

227
00:14:26,380 --> 00:14:32,840
We're not going to find a barrel of peg legs or pirate hooks or parrot skeletons or anything.

228
00:14:33,100 --> 00:14:34,240
We're not going to find that.

229
00:14:34,241 --> 00:14:40,740
What we are going to find is a ship about the right size, heavily armed,

230
00:14:41,140 --> 00:14:45,480
and having artifacts that date either before or to 1718.

231
00:14:46,720 --> 00:14:51,780
To date, the archaeology that has been performed on the Beaufort Inland Wreck

232
00:14:51,781 --> 00:14:56,680
all would tend to support the idea that this is the Queen Anne's Revenge,

233
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:00,260
but we need to keep vigilant as we test that hypothesis.

234
00:15:00,261 --> 00:15:03,500
Researchers pay special attention to the guns brought up

235
00:15:03,600 --> 00:15:04,700
from the wreck.

236
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:06,700
And this would please the pirates of old,

237
00:15:06,800 --> 00:15:09,400
because of all the equipment aboard a pirate ship,

238
00:15:09,500 --> 00:15:12,700
none was so carefully looked after as the artillery.

239
00:15:16,400 --> 00:15:19,920
Pirates were firearms masters.

240
00:15:20,020 --> 00:15:22,320
But the way guns were handled on a ship differed

241
00:15:22,420 --> 00:15:23,720
from their use on shore.

242
00:15:27,320 --> 00:15:29,720
Even the terminology used for them on board

243
00:15:29,721 --> 00:15:31,720
on board a vessel was different.

244
00:15:31,721 --> 00:15:34,720
On land, a cannon was called a cannon.

245
00:15:34,721 --> 00:15:37,720
As soon as it hit the deck of a ship, it was called a gun.

246
00:15:37,721 --> 00:15:40,720
Cannonballs were cannonballs on land.

247
00:15:40,721 --> 00:15:43,720
When it got onto a ship, it was called shot.

248
00:15:43,721 --> 00:15:47,720
The ultimate weapons on a pirate ship were the big deck guns.

249
00:15:47,721 --> 00:15:49,720
The more, the better.

250
00:15:49,721 --> 00:15:52,720
A pirate ship often had a variety of sizes

251
00:15:52,721 --> 00:15:54,720
because most of the guns were stolen

252
00:15:54,721 --> 00:15:56,720
from ships built in different countries

253
00:15:56,721 --> 00:15:59,720
by manufacturers with varying standards.

254
00:15:59,721 --> 00:16:02,160
You might have several different calibers.

255
00:16:03,300 --> 00:16:05,460
If they're capturing them, if they're stealing them,

256
00:16:05,760 --> 00:16:07,260
you can't say, well, gee, it would be nice

257
00:16:07,261 --> 00:16:09,300
if we all had the same caliber guns

258
00:16:09,301 --> 00:16:11,620
so that we only have to have one kind of ammunition.

259
00:16:12,160 --> 00:16:14,260
Sometimes you just got the cannons that you got,

260
00:16:14,360 --> 00:16:16,200
and so you might have a variety of ammunition.

261
00:16:17,280 --> 00:16:20,640
The pirates used an ingenious variety of shot in their guns.

262
00:16:22,380 --> 00:16:24,440
There's a lot more you can shoot out of cannons

263
00:16:24,441 --> 00:16:25,580
than just cannonballs.

264
00:16:25,840 --> 00:16:28,040
Everything sitting here on the deck in front of me

265
00:16:28,041 --> 00:16:30,780
is intended to be fired out of a cannon.

266
00:16:31,300 --> 00:16:33,140
Let's take, for example, chain shot.

267
00:16:33,680 --> 00:16:36,540
It's two cannonballs connected by a short length of chain,

268
00:16:36,900 --> 00:16:39,140
which when compressed in the bore of the gun

269
00:16:39,141 --> 00:16:42,040
and fired due to its irregular flight,

270
00:16:42,041 --> 00:16:46,660
will extend back out and go end over end in flight,

271
00:16:46,940 --> 00:16:48,520
tearing up anything it rips into.

272
00:16:48,680 --> 00:16:52,220
It will wrap itself around spars and snap them like twigs.

273
00:16:52,700 --> 00:16:55,640
Very close cousin to chain shot is bar shot.

274
00:16:55,641 --> 00:16:59,540
Bar shot is nothing more than two halves of the same cannonball,

275
00:16:59,940 --> 00:17:02,680
extended with a bar, cast as one piece,

276
00:17:03,080 --> 00:17:04,820
loaded the same way as chain shot.

277
00:17:05,040 --> 00:17:06,300
We'll do the same thing.

278
00:17:07,340 --> 00:17:10,940
Ironically, even though most pirate ships wielded great firepower,

279
00:17:11,560 --> 00:17:13,660
their game was not to sink an enemy vessel,

280
00:17:14,100 --> 00:17:18,600
but instead to stop it by attacking its sails and rigging.

281
00:17:20,820 --> 00:17:22,620
What good would it be to sink a ship

282
00:17:22,621 --> 00:17:25,100
if your goal was to steal the treasure it had on board?

283
00:17:25,640 --> 00:17:28,000
They were looking to capture ships,

284
00:17:28,520 --> 00:17:33,080
so utilizing these smaller anti-personnel-type weapons

285
00:17:33,081 --> 00:17:36,740
and anti-rigging-type weapons,

286
00:17:36,900 --> 00:17:38,600
firing those sorts of projectiles

287
00:17:38,601 --> 00:17:41,720
would have been much more conducive to capturing vessels

288
00:17:41,721 --> 00:17:43,820
than the hull-piercing capability

289
00:17:43,821 --> 00:17:47,220
of these carriage-mounted guns to sink ships,

290
00:17:47,340 --> 00:17:49,560
which, of course, they were not trying to do.

291
00:17:50,440 --> 00:17:52,420
There were shots specially designed

292
00:17:52,421 --> 00:17:54,760
to take out almost anything on an enemy ship.

293
00:17:55,640 --> 00:17:57,040
This is a scrapeshot.

294
00:17:57,041 --> 00:17:59,040
This is made for a larger gun.

295
00:17:59,041 --> 00:18:01,540
This is a stack of smaller cannonballs

296
00:18:01,541 --> 00:18:03,440
meant to fit into a cannon.

297
00:18:03,441 --> 00:18:06,180
The diameter of this piece of wood on top here,

298
00:18:06,181 --> 00:18:09,680
the act of firing this, would shatter the wood

299
00:18:09,681 --> 00:18:12,960
as these balls left the bore, tearing apart heavier

300
00:18:12,961 --> 00:18:16,260
portions of the ship, the side, the railings, gun ports,

301
00:18:16,261 --> 00:18:19,600
to get to the men inside the boat.

302
00:18:19,601 --> 00:18:22,440
A very odd-looking and particularly nasty weapon

303
00:18:22,441 --> 00:18:26,500
is the language or fire arrow.

304
00:18:26,501 --> 00:18:29,460
You'll notice we have a funny dart looking thing.

305
00:18:29,461 --> 00:18:32,060
This is filled with a highly combustible material,

306
00:18:32,061 --> 00:18:35,200
sulfur, a little bit of gunpowder, saltpeter, anything

307
00:18:35,201 --> 00:18:37,480
that'll burn for a length of time.

308
00:18:37,481 --> 00:18:39,440
It has this arrow point on the end

309
00:18:39,441 --> 00:18:43,060
meant to stick in a hard, solid wood surface like a mast

310
00:18:43,061 --> 00:18:44,680
or the side of the ship.

311
00:18:44,681 --> 00:18:47,720
Then you have these four chains, these double prong hooks

312
00:18:47,721 --> 00:18:50,060
meant to catch in rigging or sails.

313
00:18:50,061 --> 00:18:51,720
And this would happen.

314
00:18:51,721 --> 00:18:56,320
This thing would instantly catch a deck or rigging or sails on fire.

315
00:18:57,680 --> 00:19:02,160
Just a couple of these things could almost ensure a victory.

316
00:19:03,300 --> 00:19:05,880
Fueling all of this artillery was gunpowder.

317
00:19:06,520 --> 00:19:10,460
The ship derives its main firepower from gunpowder.

318
00:19:11,080 --> 00:19:16,380
Invented by the Chinese thousands of years before the Western world ever heard about it.

319
00:19:16,381 --> 00:19:21,580
By the age of piracy, this was the main implement that these sea battles were fought with.

320
00:19:21,720 --> 00:19:24,580
Not much by itself like that, but I'll tell you what.

321
00:19:26,820 --> 00:19:32,280
You take that gunpowder, you pack enough of it in a cloth bag like this,

322
00:19:32,740 --> 00:19:35,100
you've got to charge for a cannon this size.

323
00:19:36,640 --> 00:19:41,460
Getting a big gun ready to fire was a complicated, time-consuming task.

324
00:19:42,740 --> 00:19:44,920
This is what a pirate ship is all about.

325
00:19:45,240 --> 00:19:45,940
It's guns.

326
00:19:46,120 --> 00:19:49,100
Everything that goes on on this boat is about these guns.

327
00:19:49,900 --> 00:19:54,480
the pre-positioning of the ship, the loading, the aiming, the firing, the maintenance of these guns.

328
00:19:55,260 --> 00:19:58,148
This is a three-pounder. It's out of battery, ready to load.

329
00:19:58,149 --> 00:19:59,640
Let me show you how that works.

330
00:20:00,180 --> 00:20:03,600
Inside this pass box is the shot and the powder charge.

331
00:20:03,601 --> 00:20:05,900
The powder charge is packaged in this

332
00:20:05,901 --> 00:20:11,340
cotton bag. Gunner would insert it into the muzzle. He'll then take the rammer,

333
00:20:12,340 --> 00:20:14,260
which is the solid wooden end of this stick,

334
00:20:14,261 --> 00:20:19,200
gently push that charge all the way down to the breach of the gun

335
00:20:19,201 --> 00:20:21,480
withdraw

336
00:20:21,481 --> 00:20:24,520
reach back into the pass box

337
00:20:24,521 --> 00:20:28,380
pull out the shot or whatever kind of ammunition they're going to shoot

338
00:20:28,381 --> 00:20:29,920
insert that

339
00:20:29,921 --> 00:20:34,020
gently seat that on top of the powder charge

340
00:20:34,021 --> 00:20:36,400
the gun would be run forward

341
00:20:36,401 --> 00:20:38,320
gun is now in battery

342
00:20:38,321 --> 00:20:40,160
have to aim it

343
00:20:40,161 --> 00:20:42,000
lift the breach into the gun

344
00:20:42,001 --> 00:20:43,820
this piece here is called a coin

345
00:20:43,821 --> 00:20:46,280
This is inserted under the barrel.

346
00:20:46,281 --> 00:20:48,760
Put it at the desired height that you want.

347
00:20:48,761 --> 00:20:52,400
You aim through the gun port, looking across the top of the tube,

348
00:20:52,401 --> 00:20:53,820
about as crude as it can be.

349
00:20:53,821 --> 00:20:57,400
Watching the natural motion of the boat up and down,

350
00:20:57,401 --> 00:21:01,640
you want to fire on the upswing, so that if you're high on your target,

351
00:21:01,641 --> 00:21:02,780
you at least go into the rigging.

352
00:21:02,781 --> 00:21:05,580
If you fire on the downswing, it will throw your shot into the water.

353
00:21:05,581 --> 00:21:09,780
At this point, the gunner will take his gunner's gimlet,

354
00:21:09,781 --> 00:21:13,540
sometimes called a prick, insert it in the vent,

355
00:21:13,541 --> 00:21:16,840
which is a small touch hole used to ignite the charge,

356
00:21:17,300 --> 00:21:18,720
break open the powder bag,

357
00:21:19,860 --> 00:21:23,540
take his horn, prime the vent slightly to make sure the seat is filled,

358
00:21:24,040 --> 00:21:26,940
take a priming quill, which is a form of fuse,

359
00:21:27,740 --> 00:21:30,000
insert it in the gun tube like this.

360
00:21:30,600 --> 00:21:33,520
Then from his assistant, he will take the Lindstock,

361
00:21:33,700 --> 00:21:35,940
which is a slow match, thread it onto a stick,

362
00:21:36,520 --> 00:21:39,680
being careful to be outside the path of the recoil of the gun.

363
00:21:40,440 --> 00:21:45,220
He'll blow on the slow match to get a nice, good, hot spark going.

364
00:21:45,620 --> 00:21:48,000
Touch it sideways to the priming quill.

365
00:21:50,100 --> 00:21:53,000
The big guns were great for softening up the enemy.

366
00:21:53,700 --> 00:21:55,360
Just before the final assault,

367
00:21:55,680 --> 00:21:58,820
the pirates would give one last volley or broadside of their guns

368
00:21:58,821 --> 00:22:02,640
before boarding to get their enemy's head down below decks

369
00:22:02,641 --> 00:22:04,780
so that they could more easily take their prize.

370
00:22:09,680 --> 00:22:18,680
But any real fighting that was going to be done

371
00:22:18,681 --> 00:22:21,920
on the other ship's deck would require cutlasses,

372
00:22:23,380 --> 00:22:25,500
pistols, and muskets.

373
00:22:27,880 --> 00:22:30,080
A shiver is a wood splinter.

374
00:22:30,820 --> 00:22:33,000
The timbers of a ship splintering in battle

375
00:22:33,001 --> 00:22:36,160
gave rise to the term shiver me timbers.

376
00:22:37,040 --> 00:22:39,700
Pirate Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

377
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,860
The wreck site of what may be Blackbeard's flagship,

378
00:22:47,120 --> 00:22:48,300
the Queen Anne's Revenge,

379
00:22:48,800 --> 00:22:51,020
is revealing some intriguing hand weapons.

380
00:22:52,180 --> 00:22:53,880
What you're looking at here is a grenade

381
00:22:53,881 --> 00:22:55,740
that could have been used by a pirate.

382
00:22:56,200 --> 00:22:58,160
This is a cast iron grenade.

383
00:22:58,280 --> 00:22:59,080
It's hollow inside.

384
00:22:59,180 --> 00:23:00,260
You can see the cast lines.

385
00:23:00,261 --> 00:23:02,060
And then this is a wood plug right here.

386
00:23:02,220 --> 00:23:04,980
You can see one that has a breakaway in here.

387
00:23:04,981 --> 00:23:06,380
Here you can see the wooden plug.

388
00:23:06,440 --> 00:23:07,400
It would extend throughout.

389
00:23:07,920 --> 00:23:10,100
Gunpowder would have been put in there before this plug,

390
00:23:10,160 --> 00:23:12,020
and a fuse would have come out of here.

391
00:23:12,680 --> 00:23:14,280
The fuse would then be lit,

392
00:23:14,740 --> 00:23:17,880
and the grenade would be thrown by hand or flung with a sling.

393
00:23:18,620 --> 00:23:20,880
Its casing would explode into countless pieces.

394
00:23:23,360 --> 00:23:25,860
But pirates had more than cast-iron grenades.

395
00:23:27,100 --> 00:23:29,660
It's made out of a glass bottle.

396
00:23:29,760 --> 00:23:31,160
You could use any type of bottle.

397
00:23:31,161 --> 00:23:33,860
It has a wooden or cork plug with a fuse.

398
00:23:33,861 --> 00:23:36,760
be filled with either just straight gunpowder.

399
00:23:36,960 --> 00:23:38,660
If you're going to use it offensively

400
00:23:38,661 --> 00:23:40,600
where you're charging and you don't want to get hit

401
00:23:40,601 --> 00:23:41,660
with your own shrapnel,

402
00:23:42,140 --> 00:23:43,820
you would just have it as a shockwave,

403
00:23:43,980 --> 00:23:45,540
a flashbang, if you will.

404
00:23:46,060 --> 00:23:48,520
If you wanted to do damage, say you're up in the tops,

405
00:23:48,620 --> 00:23:50,060
you were throwing these down on the deck,

406
00:23:50,420 --> 00:23:52,200
could fill it full of pistol balls,

407
00:23:52,560 --> 00:23:54,040
pour gunpowder in around them,

408
00:23:54,041 --> 00:23:56,040
and then put your fuse in so that it would burst,

409
00:23:56,440 --> 00:23:58,520
throwing the pistol balls around as shrapnel.

410
00:23:59,180 --> 00:24:01,480
A precursor to the tear gas canister

411
00:24:01,481 --> 00:24:03,260
was the pirate's stink pot.

412
00:24:03,860 --> 00:24:09,240
They would fill a glass or a ceramic container full of rotten meat,

413
00:24:09,360 --> 00:24:13,440
anything that stank, and they would throw it over to the other ship.

414
00:24:13,540 --> 00:24:18,460
When it exploded, the stink would be so horrendous and foul

415
00:24:18,461 --> 00:24:21,720
that men actually would jump overboard to get away from it.

416
00:24:23,320 --> 00:24:25,940
And what would a pirate be without his sword?

417
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:33,040
In the earliest days of piracy, those men that had weapons preferred the rapier.

418
00:24:33,041 --> 00:24:35,440
This is what they used on land. This is what they were used to.

419
00:24:35,580 --> 00:24:39,720
A long, thin, finely made, well-balanced weapon.

420
00:24:41,920 --> 00:24:46,600
However, on the confined deck of a ship, the rapier was just about useless.

421
00:24:47,200 --> 00:24:50,740
The problem is it's too long. You can't get around without hitting something.

422
00:24:51,280 --> 00:24:53,360
Get it stuck in the deck, it's easily broken.

423
00:24:53,760 --> 00:24:55,700
They needed another weapon to replace this.

424
00:24:56,260 --> 00:24:58,780
The answer for that was the cutlass.

425
00:24:58,781 --> 00:25:03,540
Short blade, easy to move around on deck anywhere

426
00:25:03,541 --> 00:25:06,220
Thick in cross section for strength

427
00:25:06,221 --> 00:25:09,220
Able to hack and thrust at the same time

428
00:25:09,221 --> 00:25:12,600
The cutlass was also good for smashing things

429
00:25:12,601 --> 00:25:16,520
An interesting weapon is the main gauche

430
00:25:16,521 --> 00:25:18,700
This is a short left-handed weapon

431
00:25:18,701 --> 00:25:23,620
A guard to protect your hand and also use offensively as well as defensively

432
00:25:23,621 --> 00:25:27,140
The main thing this allows you to do is two-handed fighting

433
00:25:27,141 --> 00:25:28,740
Put a cutlass in the other hand.

434
00:25:29,340 --> 00:25:32,000
Now you can whirl away on the deck of a boat

435
00:25:32,001 --> 00:25:36,800
because you are still short enough to get around in any area you have to

436
00:25:36,801 --> 00:25:38,160
and be twice as lethal.

437
00:25:38,820 --> 00:25:42,560
Any pirate worth his bucket of blood needed a dagger as well.

438
00:25:42,980 --> 00:25:46,460
This is about the most common type there was during this time period.

439
00:25:46,680 --> 00:25:47,980
It's called a plug bayonet.

440
00:25:48,600 --> 00:25:49,720
It's a tapered handle.

441
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:53,660
It's intended to be inserted in the barrel of a musket after it's fired.

442
00:25:53,661 --> 00:25:56,160
It's one and only shot that you get with a musket

443
00:25:56,161 --> 00:25:59,100
to enable the weapon to still be used as a bayonet,

444
00:25:59,620 --> 00:26:00,900
a pike, that sort of instrument.

445
00:26:01,460 --> 00:26:02,800
At the Blackbeard wreck site,

446
00:26:03,280 --> 00:26:06,780
researchers are finding evidence of the pirates' use of small firearms.

447
00:26:07,440 --> 00:26:10,500
We have found over 15,000 lead shot on site.

448
00:26:10,760 --> 00:26:13,420
Here are some of the smaller end lead shot that we have found.

449
00:26:14,960 --> 00:26:17,760
And we also have some lead shot on the larger end,

450
00:26:17,820 --> 00:26:19,180
which could have been used for muskets.

451
00:26:20,600 --> 00:26:24,120
Contrary to the popular image of pirates as swashbuckling swordsmen,

452
00:26:24,121 --> 00:26:27,560
They really preferred firearms.

453
00:26:27,561 --> 00:26:31,000
Most men did not know how to use a sword.

454
00:26:31,001 --> 00:26:35,000
Only nobility were taught the art of sword play.

455
00:26:35,001 --> 00:26:37,640
So if you ended up with a cutlass in your hand,

456
00:26:37,641 --> 00:26:40,880
you had to be taught by somebody with experience,

457
00:26:40,881 --> 00:26:43,400
or you had to learn as you went.

458
00:26:43,401 --> 00:26:46,640
It was a slashing and hacking weapon,

459
00:26:46,641 --> 00:26:48,640
and you could get very tired,

460
00:26:48,641 --> 00:26:51,880
and basically whoever tired first lost.

461
00:26:51,881 --> 00:26:55,680
With a pistol, it was a much more equalizing weapon.

462
00:26:56,120 --> 00:26:59,500
If you were a good shot, you were much more deadly.

463
00:27:00,300 --> 00:27:05,100
The pirates had a wide array of firearms, each designed for a specific job.

464
00:27:06,380 --> 00:27:12,520
First, as you near your enemy target, you might send your men up into the tops, top of the mast with the musket.

465
00:27:13,120 --> 00:27:18,660
Long barrel, .75 caliber, able to reach out 100, 150 yards.

466
00:27:18,661 --> 00:27:22,900
get your best marksman, try and pick off the officers on the opposing ship.

467
00:27:24,280 --> 00:27:28,260
Although the weapons appear to be quite different and have different purposes,

468
00:27:28,800 --> 00:27:31,720
they're all flintlocks and are loaded the same way.

469
00:27:32,340 --> 00:27:35,060
First step you're going to do is put it on half-cock.

470
00:27:35,180 --> 00:27:36,220
That's the first click.

471
00:27:36,560 --> 00:27:39,280
That's where the phrase, don't go off half-cocked comes from.

472
00:27:39,800 --> 00:27:42,900
Now you take your paper cartridge, your gunpowder,

473
00:27:43,680 --> 00:27:45,660
wrapped in a paper cartridge with a ball,

474
00:27:45,661 --> 00:27:47,980
the caliber of the weapon at the end of the paper tube.

475
00:27:48,660 --> 00:27:52,100
You take the end opposite the ball, tear it off with your teeth.

476
00:27:54,380 --> 00:27:56,060
Take the first little bit of powder.

477
00:27:56,720 --> 00:27:57,900
You're priming the pan.

478
00:27:59,160 --> 00:28:00,280
Close the frizzin.

479
00:28:01,400 --> 00:28:06,760
When the flint hits the frizzin and sparks, setting off the powder we just put in,

480
00:28:07,420 --> 00:28:09,600
that will detonate the powder.

481
00:28:09,601 --> 00:28:11,300
We're going to now dump down the bore.

482
00:28:14,340 --> 00:28:16,840
All that's left now is the ball on the end.

483
00:28:16,841 --> 00:28:20,780
and shove that down into the bore part way.

484
00:28:21,220 --> 00:28:22,380
Take out your ramrod.

485
00:28:25,060 --> 00:28:26,500
Gently tamp that home.

486
00:28:26,620 --> 00:28:28,260
Push it all the way down to the base.

487
00:28:30,700 --> 00:28:31,900
Withdraw the ramrod.

488
00:28:32,780 --> 00:28:35,460
By the way, you're expected to do this three times a minute

489
00:28:35,461 --> 00:28:36,600
if you're a good marksman.

490
00:28:38,440 --> 00:28:39,920
Now we're ready to fire.

491
00:28:40,360 --> 00:28:42,260
Pull the hammer back to the second click.

492
00:28:42,800 --> 00:28:43,960
The safety is off.

493
00:28:44,340 --> 00:28:46,400
All I have to do now is aim and shoot.

494
00:28:46,840 --> 00:28:53,840
A pirate preferred his short-range blunderbuss

495
00:28:53,841 --> 00:28:54,840
when boarding a ship.

496
00:28:54,841 --> 00:28:56,840
Fast to load, a huge bore.

497
00:28:56,841 --> 00:28:58,840
You can't miss throwing the shot in there.

498
00:28:58,841 --> 00:29:01,840
It is the equivalent of five or six guys firing pistols

499
00:29:01,841 --> 00:29:02,840
at the same time.

500
00:29:02,841 --> 00:29:05,840
Really a devastating weapon.

501
00:29:05,841 --> 00:29:07,840
At extremely close range,

502
00:29:07,841 --> 00:29:10,840
the ship was a very strong, very strong, very strong,

503
00:29:10,841 --> 00:29:13,840
very strong, very strong, very strong.

504
00:29:13,841 --> 00:29:18,060
At extremely close range, a pirate used his pistol.

505
00:29:18,840 --> 00:29:21,060
He often carried more than one of them into battle.

506
00:29:21,840 --> 00:29:24,660
Obviously, it takes a little bit of time to reload a flintlock.

507
00:29:25,200 --> 00:29:28,720
Another way around this was to take three of these single-shot pistols,

508
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:30,140
tie them around your neck.

509
00:29:30,680 --> 00:29:34,300
As you board that ship, trying to make the bridgehead, if you will,

510
00:29:34,360 --> 00:29:36,060
for the rest of your crew coming behind you,

511
00:29:36,300 --> 00:29:39,900
you've got three shots right there that you can use to clear out a space

512
00:29:39,901 --> 00:29:42,740
to get your men on board that prize that you want so much

513
00:29:42,741 --> 00:29:44,020
without reloading.

514
00:29:44,860 --> 00:29:47,480
Yet all the firepower in the world was of no use

515
00:29:47,481 --> 00:29:50,840
if buccaneers weren't in the right place at the right time.

516
00:29:52,300 --> 00:29:54,320
Pirate ships depended on their navigators

517
00:29:54,321 --> 00:29:56,400
to strategically position them for action.

518
00:29:57,480 --> 00:30:00,940
The best navigators were literally worth their weight in gold

519
00:30:00,941 --> 00:30:04,900
when charting a ship toward treasure or away from danger.

520
00:30:07,620 --> 00:30:10,040
Early cannon shot was made of stone

521
00:30:10,041 --> 00:30:12,440
that was hand-cut by skilled artisans.

522
00:30:12,740 --> 00:30:17,880
By the 1600s, iron shot was mass-produced cheaper and faster,

523
00:30:18,560 --> 00:30:22,580
and thus became the preferred projectile for maritime artillerists.

524
00:30:23,800 --> 00:30:26,660
Pirate Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

525
00:30:29,900 --> 00:30:34,060
Finding treasure would be easy if X always marked the spot.

526
00:30:34,380 --> 00:30:38,160
But in reality, pirates used navigators to lead them to the riches.

527
00:30:38,161 --> 00:30:46,160
Apart from the captain, your navigator would probably be your most important person aboard a ship.

528
00:30:46,161 --> 00:30:53,160
You needed to know not only where you were going, but how to get there and what sort of obstacles would be in your way.

529
00:30:53,161 --> 00:30:57,113
How deep was the water? How treacherous were the shoals?

530
00:30:57,114 --> 00:30:59,160
Were there reefs in the area?

531
00:30:59,161 --> 00:31:07,160
You needed to be able to avoid those perils or else it didn't matter whether you caught any ships or not if you sank your own.

532
00:31:07,161 --> 00:31:10,160
The job required a man of many skills.

533
00:31:10,161 --> 00:31:13,160
He had to be literate.

534
00:31:13,161 --> 00:31:15,160
He needed to know mathematics.

535
00:31:15,161 --> 00:31:18,160
He needed to be able to read the instruments.

536
00:31:18,161 --> 00:31:21,160
The navigation instruments the pirates used were stolen

537
00:31:21,161 --> 00:31:24,160
from ships of different cultures.

538
00:31:24,161 --> 00:31:26,160
They took the best of each of these

539
00:31:26,161 --> 00:31:29,160
and adapted them for their own use.

540
00:31:29,161 --> 00:31:32,160
Well, the pirates would certainly utilize whatever technology

541
00:31:32,161 --> 00:31:35,160
they were able to steal or plunder.

542
00:31:35,161 --> 00:31:39,160
In a lot of cases, particularly with these navigational instruments, they would certainly

543
00:31:39,161 --> 00:31:43,060
utilize whatever they found to work best for them.

544
00:31:43,061 --> 00:31:50,160
One of the jobs of the navigator, if he was very good, was knowing the trade routes, where

545
00:31:50,161 --> 00:31:56,720
they would be at certain times of the year, so that he could navigate the ship very close

546
00:31:56,721 --> 00:32:03,680
to where they assumed the ship should be, and also to know the best escape route from

547
00:32:03,681 --> 00:32:04,680
that area.

548
00:32:04,681 --> 00:32:08,680
Many of the pirates' navigation devices are still in use.

549
00:32:08,780 --> 00:32:12,480
The most important tool in navigation aboard any ship today

550
00:32:12,580 --> 00:32:14,720
or in the golden age of sail is the ship's compass.

551
00:32:14,820 --> 00:32:16,720
It's always located here by the ship's helm,

552
00:32:16,820 --> 00:32:20,220
so the helmsman knows what direction to sail the ship.

553
00:32:20,320 --> 00:32:24,840
At the very front of the compass is the lubber line.

554
00:32:24,940 --> 00:32:28,040
It's a fixed line indicator pointing toward the exact front

555
00:32:28,140 --> 00:32:32,240
of the ship and is therefore the zero point from which relative

556
00:32:32,241 --> 00:32:33,900
which relative bearings are measured.

557
00:32:37,760 --> 00:32:40,380
This is one of the more obvious instruments or tools

558
00:32:40,381 --> 00:32:43,640
that a navigator would use as a spyglass or a telescope.

559
00:32:43,641 --> 00:32:46,280
It's used to get a good fix on a piece of land

560
00:32:46,281 --> 00:32:50,000
or a ship that the observer will be using to navigate by.

561
00:32:50,001 --> 00:32:52,340
It's usually used in the hand, so it

562
00:32:52,341 --> 00:32:55,440
can compensate for the pitching of the ship back and forth.

563
00:32:55,441 --> 00:32:57,840
But when you're at a still point of view,

564
00:32:57,841 --> 00:33:00,680
you can put on a tripod so it'll stay still

565
00:33:00,681 --> 00:33:04,100
and get an idea of what you're looking at a little bit better.

566
00:33:04,760 --> 00:33:06,660
The spyglass was very important

567
00:33:06,661 --> 00:33:10,860
because much of the sailing at this time was by line of sight,

568
00:33:11,780 --> 00:33:14,980
meaning pirates would attempt to use any islands, reefs,

569
00:33:15,100 --> 00:33:17,480
or other natural landmarks to get their bearings.

570
00:33:20,240 --> 00:33:23,860
The spyglass gave them a closer look at these landmarks,

571
00:33:23,861 --> 00:33:25,760
allowing for more accurate navigation.

572
00:33:25,761 --> 00:33:32,500
measuring the speed of the ship gave them a clearer idea of how far they had traveled

573
00:33:32,501 --> 00:33:38,940
and how much longer they would be en route this is a device known as a chip log it's an instrument

574
00:33:38,941 --> 00:33:42,860
that the sailors would use to determine the ship's speed they would toss the chip overboard

575
00:33:42,861 --> 00:33:50,260
at which point the line would start paying out and uh one of the sailors would have the line in his

576
00:33:50,261 --> 00:33:54,460
hand letting the line run through his hands like this till the first line would first knot would

577
00:33:54,461 --> 00:33:58,680
past his hand right here, you tell one of the other sailors, Mark, he turned the sand glass over.

578
00:33:59,360 --> 00:34:04,660
Then he'd start to count how many knots on the line would pass through his hands in the amount

579
00:34:04,661 --> 00:34:06,848
of time for the sand glass to run out.

580
00:34:06,849 --> 00:34:09,900
And as soon as the sand glass has run out, he'd stop,

581
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:13,878
he'd hold the line, and he'd count how many knots have run through his hand.

582
00:34:13,879 --> 00:34:15,000
And however many knots

583
00:34:15,001 --> 00:34:18,760
has run through his hand, would they determine how many nautical miles an hour the ship would be

584
00:34:18,761 --> 00:34:22,200
going, and that's where we get the term knots.

585
00:34:22,201 --> 00:34:26,060
Some of their navigation tools were very simple, yet very

586
00:34:26,061 --> 00:34:27,260
effective.

587
00:34:27,261 --> 00:34:30,700
This is a fairly common object on a lot of shipwrecks.

588
00:34:30,701 --> 00:34:32,240
It's called a sounding weight.

589
00:34:32,241 --> 00:34:36,800
Had a rope tied here, had been chunked in, hit the bottom,

590
00:34:36,801 --> 00:34:39,780
and gave the mariners an idea of the depth of the water

591
00:34:39,781 --> 00:34:41,540
that they were sailing in.

592
00:34:41,541 --> 00:34:44,580
I would make note of these marks going through.

593
00:34:44,581 --> 00:34:46,940
These marks are one fathom apart.

594
00:34:46,941 --> 00:34:50,006
the line went slack at the end of three of these marks.

595
00:34:50,007 --> 00:34:52,180
That means that I'm three fathoms or 18

596
00:34:52,181 --> 00:34:53,304
feet to the bottom.

597
00:34:53,305 --> 00:34:57,920
I would bring it back up and if I had sand here I'd know the bottom was sandy,

598
00:34:58,280 --> 00:35:00,821
little rocks I'd know it had rocks in it.

599
00:35:00,822 --> 00:35:03,920
If nothing came back up I would know probably that

600
00:35:03,921 --> 00:35:06,149
it was solid rock of some type.

601
00:35:06,150 --> 00:35:10,320
They used a quadrant to determine latitude which was their

602
00:35:10,321 --> 00:35:12,981
position north or south of the equator.

603
00:35:12,982 --> 00:35:16,120
The instrument was designed to measure angular

604
00:35:16,121 --> 00:35:18,560
distances using the sun as a reference point.

605
00:35:19,520 --> 00:35:21,300
The way it works is this.

606
00:35:21,780 --> 00:35:28,320
The navigator would look directly at the sun and line up this edge with the sun.

607
00:35:28,620 --> 00:35:34,060
They could then have somebody else come along and tell what mark the string was at on the

608
00:35:34,061 --> 00:35:36,040
quadrant, which would give the angle.

609
00:35:36,500 --> 00:35:40,740
What they'd do is start before no noon, and they'd watch that sun go up.

610
00:35:40,820 --> 00:35:44,700
They'd see the angle increasing, and then after a point, it would start decreasing.

611
00:35:44,701 --> 00:35:47,980
That greatest angle was noon, was always.

612
00:35:48,100 --> 00:35:50,440
They didn't need a timepiece in order to determine that.

613
00:35:50,860 --> 00:35:53,640
And then from that angle, they would go to some books they had

614
00:35:53,641 --> 00:35:56,380
and determine what was the latitude of their position.

615
00:35:56,740 --> 00:36:00,760
The trouble was they had to look for long periods of time directly into the sun,

616
00:36:00,860 --> 00:36:04,140
which, of course, was very detrimental to their eyes.

617
00:36:05,220 --> 00:36:06,880
Finding latitude was important.

618
00:36:07,680 --> 00:36:10,560
However, pinpoint nautical accuracy didn't come about

619
00:36:10,561 --> 00:36:14,520
until instruments were devised that would also accurately measure longitude.

620
00:36:14,700 --> 00:36:18,700
These weren't developed until the late 18th century,

621
00:36:18,740 --> 00:36:21,700
after the Great Age of Piracy.

622
00:36:21,740 --> 00:36:24,700
Therefore, the most coveted of all navigation aids available

623
00:36:24,740 --> 00:36:27,700
to pirates were sea charts.

624
00:36:27,740 --> 00:36:31,700
The charts were made by the histories that were given

625
00:36:31,740 --> 00:36:34,700
by other captains, and they told you things like how deep the

626
00:36:34,740 --> 00:36:38,700
water was, where the reefs were, where the land masses were.

627
00:36:38,740 --> 00:36:41,700
They had orientation on them that you could use to find where

628
00:36:41,701 --> 00:36:45,180
to find where you were and to locate yourself on the chart

629
00:36:45,181 --> 00:36:47,700
relative to the land masses and to the reefs

630
00:36:47,701 --> 00:36:48,900
and that kind of thing.

631
00:36:50,000 --> 00:36:52,380
Accurate charts could be more valuable than treasure.

632
00:36:53,580 --> 00:36:55,960
When you captured a ship, part of the loot

633
00:36:55,961 --> 00:36:58,120
would be any sort of navigational charts

634
00:36:58,121 --> 00:37:00,500
that this other ship had on board.

635
00:37:00,501 --> 00:37:03,780
And oftentimes, you would have captains

636
00:37:03,781 --> 00:37:05,780
throwing them overboard so that the pirates

637
00:37:05,781 --> 00:37:06,520
couldn't take them.

638
00:37:06,700 --> 00:37:09,760
That was certainly one of the biggest prizes to be taken,

639
00:37:09,761 --> 00:37:14,040
and that let you sail around areas that might have been more difficult otherwise.

640
00:37:14,300 --> 00:37:18,380
A good navigator could bring a pirate ship to a galleon packed with treasure.

641
00:37:19,320 --> 00:37:22,620
But the thrill of bloody battle left many a pirate wounded,

642
00:37:23,240 --> 00:37:28,860
and the medical attention they received aboard ship could be more painful than the wound itself.

643
00:37:31,580 --> 00:37:36,760
Many pirates were superstitious and considered the compass to have magical properties.

644
00:37:36,761 --> 00:37:40,560
To ease their fears, they housed the compass in a small wooden

645
00:37:40,660 --> 00:37:45,260
box called a binnacle, a term still in use today.

646
00:37:45,360 --> 00:37:48,200
Pirate Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

647
00:37:57,120 --> 00:37:59,720
Some of the medical equipment found at the Queen Anne's

648
00:37:59,820 --> 00:38:02,020
revenge site is surprisingly sophisticated.

649
00:38:02,120 --> 00:38:04,820
I don't want to excavate too much more

650
00:38:04,821 --> 00:38:07,620
much more with this large dread.

651
00:38:07,621 --> 00:38:11,700
This is a small pewter urethral syringe,

652
00:38:11,701 --> 00:38:14,560
a little bit crushed probably during the wrecking event.

653
00:38:14,561 --> 00:38:16,260
See the tapered point up here?

654
00:38:16,261 --> 00:38:18,640
And would have been utilized for treating

655
00:38:18,641 --> 00:38:20,600
probably venereal diseases.

656
00:38:20,601 --> 00:38:24,000
There was a very minute amount of mercury on the inside,

657
00:38:24,001 --> 00:38:27,740
which was utilized for treating venereal diseases

658
00:38:27,741 --> 00:38:29,580
during this period.

659
00:38:29,581 --> 00:38:33,180
Yet most onboard medical care was primitive.

660
00:38:33,181 --> 00:38:35,180
Amputations were especially feared.

661
00:38:35,181 --> 00:38:40,720
Laid out here in front of me is a very rare surgical kit used for amputations and orthopedic

662
00:38:40,721 --> 00:38:41,720
needs.

663
00:38:41,721 --> 00:38:43,080
It's dated 1750.

664
00:38:43,081 --> 00:38:46,240
Inside this kit here we have lots of different tools that are really a primitive version

665
00:38:46,241 --> 00:38:48,160
of what we use today.

666
00:38:48,161 --> 00:38:52,680
The bow saw here would have been used to amputate a larger limb such as a leg or an arm and

667
00:38:52,681 --> 00:38:56,700
the patients may have had to been tied down or pinned down by other pirates on the ship

668
00:38:56,701 --> 00:38:59,500
in order to saw off the entire leg.

669
00:38:59,501 --> 00:39:03,380
On pirate ships, a lot of times the doctors and carpenters would have been the same people

670
00:39:03,381 --> 00:39:04,720
or using the same tools.

671
00:39:04,860 --> 00:39:08,260
Therefore, if one of the doctors wasn't around, then the carpenter may just have to use the

672
00:39:08,261 --> 00:39:09,220
saw to cut off a limb.

673
00:39:09,520 --> 00:39:12,440
So it wasn't always an educated doctor that would have done the surgeries.

674
00:39:13,140 --> 00:39:18,480
I would imagine any sort of amputation during this period would have been very difficult

675
00:39:18,481 --> 00:39:19,240
to watch.

676
00:39:19,660 --> 00:39:24,240
Basically, the only anesthesia that would have been available would have been several shots

677
00:39:24,241 --> 00:39:26,640
of the hardest liquor that they had on board.

678
00:39:26,641 --> 00:39:31,640
This here is an amputation knife, similar to what would have been used with the bow

679
00:39:31,641 --> 00:39:32,341
saw.

680
00:39:32,342 --> 00:39:36,640
This would have been used for smaller appendages such as fingers or toes, maybe ears,

681
00:39:36,641 --> 00:39:41,640
but this would have been one of the more commonly used instruments within the surgical kit.

682
00:39:41,641 --> 00:39:46,640
With all the fighting pirates had to face, it wasn't uncommon to see men without limbs.

683
00:39:46,641 --> 00:39:52,844
The image of a pirate with a hook or a peg leg is not a cliche or a fallacy.

684
00:39:52,845 --> 00:39:54,640
They were used in that

685
00:39:54,641 --> 00:40:02,720
time not just by pirates but by other individuals a hook was very expensive it was usually made out

686
00:40:02,721 --> 00:40:12,000
of a leather base attached to the arm and a peg leg would either be carved to fit their kneecap or

687
00:40:12,001 --> 00:40:18,080
higher up or they would just take a very heavy stick and tie it with rope or leather around

688
00:40:18,081 --> 00:40:21,320
stump of their leg.

689
00:40:21,321 --> 00:40:24,080
If pirates couldn't count on good medical care,

690
00:40:24,081 --> 00:40:26,860
they couldn't count on a hearty meal either.

691
00:40:26,861 --> 00:40:30,200
Provisions spoiled quickly.

692
00:40:30,201 --> 00:40:32,460
Often they turned to preserved foods,

693
00:40:32,461 --> 00:40:35,900
like tough salted pork.

694
00:40:35,901 --> 00:40:37,640
We have recovered a number of pewter artifacts

695
00:40:37,641 --> 00:40:39,160
from our shipwreck.

696
00:40:39,161 --> 00:40:40,800
What's particular about this pewter plate

697
00:40:40,801 --> 00:40:43,940
is you can see the cut marks that are on this plate,

698
00:40:43,941 --> 00:40:47,140
which could be an indication of how they had to cut their meat.

699
00:40:47,141 --> 00:40:49,660
and the salted pork was actually pretty tough.

700
00:40:50,100 --> 00:40:52,620
So this could be an indication of trying to get through that meat.

701
00:40:54,320 --> 00:40:57,020
There's an irony in the pirates' poor food quality,

702
00:40:57,540 --> 00:41:01,740
because the word buccaneer is derived from the French word boucan,

703
00:41:02,080 --> 00:41:04,200
which is a smokehouse for preserving meat.

704
00:41:05,540 --> 00:41:09,120
On land, some of the men who became pirates worked in such smokehouses.

705
00:41:09,500 --> 00:41:14,000
Because they reeked of the smoke, people began calling them boucaniers,

706
00:41:14,001 --> 00:41:17,540
which later became Buccaneer.

707
00:41:20,180 --> 00:41:22,380
Some hard-luck crews were even forced

708
00:41:22,381 --> 00:41:24,320
to eat the rats on board to survive.

709
00:41:25,480 --> 00:41:30,340
A lot of times, rats ended up being the only protein

710
00:41:30,341 --> 00:41:32,000
that you could find on a ship.

711
00:41:32,380 --> 00:41:34,560
All the food was stored in wood barrels,

712
00:41:35,100 --> 00:41:39,480
and because of the salt and the humidity and other factors,

713
00:41:39,700 --> 00:41:41,120
food went bad very quickly,

714
00:41:41,121 --> 00:41:45,980
so they would even eat the weevils and the maggots that were in the food,

715
00:41:46,300 --> 00:41:48,220
whatever they needed to do to survive.

716
00:41:49,180 --> 00:41:52,280
And a pirate's bottle of rum was more than a cliché.

717
00:41:52,920 --> 00:41:54,520
It, too, was a matter of survival.

718
00:41:55,000 --> 00:41:58,560
They all would drink a lot, and that's probably because the water was so horrible.

719
00:41:58,660 --> 00:42:01,720
You put water in a barrel and keep it on your boat for a while,

720
00:42:02,200 --> 00:42:06,160
it gets slimy and hard to drink.

721
00:42:06,480 --> 00:42:10,000
So, of course, they're going to drink anything else that they have on hand that keeps better,

722
00:42:10,001 --> 00:42:12,260
and alcohol is one of those things that keeps pretty well.

723
00:42:13,500 --> 00:42:16,720
They'd often add rum to available water to make it safer.

724
00:42:17,380 --> 00:42:19,420
This concoction was called grog.

725
00:42:21,360 --> 00:42:23,060
And as for the bodily functions?

726
00:42:24,600 --> 00:42:27,180
This is called a pisdell or pissois,

727
00:42:27,660 --> 00:42:29,620
known in modern times as the urinal.

728
00:42:29,920 --> 00:42:32,800
It's made out of lead, but it's crushed, as you can tell.

729
00:42:33,180 --> 00:42:35,440
So actually what you're looking at is Blackbeard's head.

730
00:42:37,160 --> 00:42:39,340
And why did they put up with all of this hardship?

731
00:42:40,000 --> 00:42:45,460
The money, of course, and maybe the challenge.

732
00:42:47,460 --> 00:42:52,000
Pirates stayed one step ahead of the law by using any technology that was innovative.

733
00:42:52,940 --> 00:42:55,500
Did they invent different things?

734
00:42:55,501 --> 00:43:03,740
I think we want to think more of them adapting different technology to their uses.

735
00:43:03,741 --> 00:43:11,560
And the fact that they captured ships from all over meant that they had a lot of different technologies at their disposal.

736
00:43:12,600 --> 00:43:16,500
They might capture a French slave ship, and so they would have French cannon.

737
00:43:16,920 --> 00:43:19,000
A Spanish ship might have other things.

738
00:43:19,220 --> 00:43:23,860
Ships coming back from the Orient might have different devices on them that they might find useful.

739
00:43:24,220 --> 00:43:31,080
The truly successful pirates were the pirates that could adapt the vessels and whatever they stole to their needs the best.

740
00:43:31,081 --> 00:43:35,140
As the 19th century neared, the pirates' days were numbered.

741
00:43:36,200 --> 00:43:42,780
Merchants and regular citizens started really pressuring their governments to do something to stamp out pirates.

742
00:43:43,020 --> 00:43:49,280
And the governments then, to save their own skins and jobs, had to start seriously capturing them

743
00:43:49,281 --> 00:43:53,800
and making a statement that pirates were no longer allowed on the waters,

744
00:43:54,200 --> 00:43:56,840
though piracy never completely ended.

745
00:43:56,841 --> 00:43:59,980
For a time, their stripped-down,

746
00:44:00,160 --> 00:44:02,280
customized ships ruled the seas.

747
00:44:02,820 --> 00:44:04,820
They were a bloody and treacherous lot,

748
00:44:05,020 --> 00:44:06,380
and they did much harm.

749
00:44:07,280 --> 00:44:09,840
Yet by adapting their ships to sail faster

750
00:44:09,841 --> 00:44:11,960
and their artillery to be more lethal,

751
00:44:12,700 --> 00:44:15,320
they pushed the envelope of marine technology.

